    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The drum and the echo"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Hit a drum and you hear it across the room. Shout towards a hill and your voice returns. Sound travels as a wave, and a few characteristics describe it completely."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "compressions and rarefactions",
          "loudness and pitch",
          "medium needed",
          "reflection and echo",
          "speed of sound",
          "use of reflection",
          "v = f lambda",
          "wavelength and frequency"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Sound is produced by vibrating objects and needs a medium such as air, water or a solid to travel. It cannot travel through a vacuum.\nSound travels as a longitudinal wave of compressions and rarefactions.\nSound waves carry energy. The speed of sound in air is about 346 m/s at 25 degrees Celsius.\nThe distance between two successive compressions is the wavelength; the number of vibrations per second is the frequency, in hertz.\nSpeed, frequency and wavelength are related: v = f x wavelength.\nAmplitude is the maximum displacement; it decides loudness. Frequency decides pitch.\nSound is reflected by hard surfaces, which gives echoes. Reflection also follows the laws of reflection.\nCurved ceilings and walls in halls reflect sound so that it reaches the audience better."
        }
